}

Now I could use a multiplier on the radius to make
r in the if statement reflect the new radius, 50, but I was wondering if there was a way that I could do it without having to actually rewrite the number value. I'm totally stumped...

Unfortunately this will not work for other scale values because your translate command is hardcoded for a scale of 2.

The problem is that the scale command will stretch/shrink the drawiang area from the top left corner so the translation command to centre the drawing coordinates would also need to be scaled. Also scale does not the affect mouseX and mouseY these will always be measured in actual pixels from the top left hand corner.

The following code does away with the need for translate and will work with any scale. Hope you are able to use the ideas in your sketch.

A more generic approach would be to consider the screen represents some area of 'world space' and then create methods to translate points from world > screen and screen > world. A shape would have a position and size in world space and the screen posiition and size would be calculated using the world > screen method when you need to draw it. To see if the mouse pointer is over a shape use the screen > world method to calculate mouseX and mouseY in world values then compare these to the shape's world position
